<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Essen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Essen is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew around Essen Abbey, a community of noble canonesses founded in the 9th century whose princess-abbesses ruled the town until 1803. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and armaments works made it one of the industrial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its former abbey church became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen-cathedral">Essen Cathedral</a> in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/essen.jpg" alt="Essen" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Essen_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Essen lies at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, the largest urban area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and is its second-largest city after Dortmund. The Emscher flows through the north of the city and the Ruhr River, dammed to form Lake Baldeney and Lake Kettwig, through the south. The oldest archaeological find is the Vogelheimer Klinge, a blade dated to between 280,000 and 250,000 BCE, found in 1926 during the building of the Rhine-Herne Canal. A megalithic tomb found in 1937 is described as Essen's earliest preserved architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 845 Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altfrid">Altfrid</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, founded a community for women, with his relative Gerswit as first abbess. It was a residence and school for the daughters and widows of the higher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility, and members other than the abbess were not obliged to take vows of chastity. In 1216 Emperor Frederick II called the abbess Elisabeth I a Princess of the Empire. Essen received a town charter in 1244, but in 1290 King Rudolph I restored the abbesses to full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Charles IV granted the title of free imperial city in 1377, yet disputes between city and abbey continued until the abbey's dissolution in 1803.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> the Protestant city and the Catholic abbey were opposed. In 1623 the princess-abbess Maria Clara von Spaur directed Catholic Spaniards against the city, and a law of 1624 aimed to make the city Catholic again, and churchgoing was strictly controlled. She fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> stormed Essen in 1629. Mining rights were granted to the abbess in the fourteenth century, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine opened in 1354 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining began in 1450. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the territory in 1802.</p><p>Industrialisation, above all the iron works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> family, made Essen one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> centres. Alfred Krupp built the mansion Villa Hügel in 1873. Heavy industry declined in the late twentieth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Essen is the tenth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with 573,618 inhabitants. The Zollverein Coal Mine Industrial Complex, whose Shaft XII is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> style, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 2001. Essen Minster, the former abbey church, keeps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-madonna-of-essen">Golden Madonna of Essen</a>, made around 980 and regarded as the oldest known sculpture of the Madonna. The city was European Capital of Culture on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Essen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A &quot;Missa greca&quot; sung at Pentecost survives in a sacramentary written for canonesses at <b>Essen</b>, with the Greek words in Latin letters and early neumes, and the Royal Abbey of Saint-Denis sang the hymn in Greek and in Latin at Pentecost and in its patron's festal week, after…</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Essen</b> Abbey was founded around 845 to 852 by Altfrid, later bishop of Hildesheim, as a community of canonesses for women of the Saxon nobility.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Romanesque church, the basilica of Saint Ludger, survives in what is now a district of <b>Essen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-werden">Imperial Abbey of Werden</a></li></ul></section>
